---
import {
  REPORTER_MIN_AGE_DAYS,
  REPORTS_TO_HIDE,
  STRIKE_DAYS,
  STRIKE_LIMIT,
  TRUST_AFTER_APPROVALS,
} from "@trilleo/storage";
import InfoLayout from "../../layouts/InfoLayout.astro";
import { COMMENT_MAX_LENGTH, LIMITS } from "../../lib/comments/store";
import {
  MC_LIMITS,
  PROJECT_REPORTS_TO_HIDE,
} from "../../lib/minecraft/catalog";

const sections = [
  { slug: "spirit", text: "The spirit" },
  { slug: "not-allowed", text: "Not allowed" },
  { slug: "comments", text: "Comments" },
  { slug: "uploads", text: "Uploads" },
  { slug: "minecraft", text: "Minecraft projects" },
  { slug: "reporting", text: "Reporting" },
  { slug: "consequences", text: "What happens when rules are broken" },
  { slug: "appeals", text: "Appeals" },
] as const;
---

<InfoLayout path="/legal/guidelines/" sections={sections} history>
  <h2 id="spirit">The spirit</h2>
  <p>
    This is a small, personal corner of the web. Comments are for talking about
    the posts; profiles and uploads are for sharing what you make. Be the kind
    of guest you’d want in your own home: curious, generous, and kind to people
    you disagree with.
  </p>

  <h2 id="not-allowed">Not allowed</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Harassment, threats, hate against people for who they are, or encouraging
      anyone to hurt themselves or others.
    </li>
    <li>Sexual content, or anything sexual involving minors, ever.</li>
    <li>
      Other people’s private information (addresses, phone numbers, documents)
      shared without their consent.
    </li>
    <li>
      Content that is illegal in mainland China, where the site is hosted, or
      where you are.
    </li>
    <li>
      Malware, phishing, cracked software, cheats for other people’s games, or
      links to them.
    </li>
    <li>
      Work you don’t have the right to share (see{" "}
      <a href="/legal/copyright/">copyright</a>).
    </li>
    <li>
      Spam, advertising, scams, or pretending to be someone else, including me.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="comments">Comments</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Your first comment is read before it appears. Once one is approved, your
      later comments appear straight away.
    </li>
    <li>
      To keep spam out, you can post {LIMITS.burst.count} comments in{" "}
      {LIMITS.burst.windowMs / 60_000} minutes, {LIMITS.daily.count} a day, and
      have {LIMITS.pending} waiting for approval at once. Comments can be up to{" "}
      {COMMENT_MAX_LENGTH.toLocaleString("en")} characters.
    </li>
    <li>
      You can delete your own comments at any time. If someone replied, a
      placeholder keeps the thread readable.
    </li>
    <li>
      Some posts close comments, or have none; that’s a choice about the post,
      not about you.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="uploads">Uploads</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Anyone signed in can share Minecraft creations; other kinds of upload are
      open only to some accounts for now. Every file is checked for malware, and
      public files are reviewed before anyone else sees them, until you are
      trusted.
    </li>
    <li>
      You become trusted automatically after {TRUST_AFTER_APPROVALS} approved
      uploads with no strikes. Trusted uploaders publish straight away, but
      their files are still spot-checked and can be reported.
    </li>
    <li>
      Mods and plugins (.jar files) are always looked at before they go out,
      however trusted you are, because people run them.
    </li>
    <li>
      Web pages, scripts and other active files are only offered as downloads,
      never opened in the browser.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="minecraft">Minecraft projects</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Describe it honestly: a name and summary that say what it is, pictures
      that show it, and the Minecraft versions and loaders it really works with.
    </li>
    <li>
      Share your own work, or work its licence lets you share. No re-uploads of
      other people’s projects, even with credit, unless their licence allows it.
    </li>
    <li>
      Nothing hidden: no code obfuscated to hide what it does, no backdoors
      (such as a plugin that gives its author operator rights), no miners, and
      no data collection or network calls a player wouldn’t expect unless the
      description says so plainly.
    </li>
    <li>
      No cheats for servers that aren’t yours, and nothing made to grief or
      crash other people’s worlds.
    </li>
    <li>
      Keep it tidy: up to {MC_LIMITS.projectsPerAccount} projects per account,{" "}
      {MC_LIMITS.gallery} pictures per project and {MC_LIMITS.releasesPerDay}{" "}
      releases a day. Put new versions in new releases of the same project,
      rather than new projects.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="reporting">Reporting</h2>
  <p>
    If something breaks these rules, report it: use “Report this file” on a
    file’s page, or <a href="/contact/">get in touch</a> about a comment or
    profile. Reports are read by a person. A public file reported by{" "}
    {REPORTS_TO_HIDE} different people whose accounts are at least{" "}
    {REPORTER_MIN_AGE_DAYS} days old is hidden until it’s been reviewed. For a
    Minecraft project’s page (its description or pictures), use “Report this
    project”; {PROJECT_REPORTS_TO_HIDE} reports hide it the same way. Please
    don’t report things only because you disagree with them: false reports slow
    everything down.
  </p>

  <h2 id="consequences">What happens when rules are broken</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Comments</strong> can be hidden. Someone who keeps breaking the
      rules can be blocked: they can no longer sign in, and everything they
      wrote is hidden.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Files</strong> can be refused or taken down. Each of those usually
      comes with a strike, which lasts {STRIKE_DAYS} days, and the same file
      can’t be uploaded again. {STRIKE_LIMIT} active strikes stop you uploading.
      Malware is refused automatically.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Minecraft projects</strong> whose pages break the rules can be
      hidden; the creator sees why on their dashboard. Their files follow the
      rules for files above.
    </li>
    <li>
      Serious or illegal content may be removed at once, without a warning, and
      reported to the authorities where the law requires it.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="appeals">Appeals</h2>
  <p>
    If you think a decision about your file was wrong, appeal it from{" "}
    <a href="/account/files/">your files</a> (once per file). While an appeal is
    open, the file isn’t deleted. An accepted appeal restores the file and
    removes the strike. For anything else, such as a hidden comment, a hidden
    project or a blocked account, <a href="/contact/">write to me</a>.
  </p>
</InfoLayout>
